  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/a/ad/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_101.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Thor is stomping through the city in a terrible mood - he <i>has </i>to see Odin.&nbsp; The ants nearby notify Hank Pym, and he (as Giant-Man) and the Wasp summon Iron Man to head out and see why Thor is in such a foul mood.&nbsp; He's paying no attention to the damage he's causing along the way!&nbsp; \"Mortals!&nbsp; How like chattering monkeys you are!\"&nbsp; (<i>Wow</i>, he's in a frightful mood!)&nbsp; He turns to the Avengers and tells them that what he has to do must be done alone!&nbsp; \"This is no concern of yours!&nbsp; I say <i>go</i>!\"&nbsp; Iron Man convinces Giant-Man that this appears to be a personal problem and that it's none of their business.&nbsp; (That may be true, but when you go around smashing things and cars, that makes it Avengers business, at least in my mind!)\n\nThor is still miffed about Odin's declaration about Jane at the end of the previous issue and that he will not grant him permission to marry Jane Foster!&nbsp; What he doesn't know is that Loki is assisting Odin in hearing Thor's thoughts.&nbsp; He's still pining over the mortal woman even though Odin has forbidden it.&nbsp; \"Is this not rank disobedience?\" Loki asks.&nbsp; Odin is manipulated into believing it, and he thus reduces Thor's power by <i>half </i>until \"he gives up all thoughts of the mortal girl he loves!\"\n\nWhen Thor approaches Heimdall for entrance into Asgard, he is <i>refused</i>!&nbsp; \"Those are the orders of Odin himself!\"&nbsp; Thor will not have it and wields his hammer to strike Heimdall, but with his power reduced, Heimdall is able to strike back!&nbsp; Thor is surprised and tries again and realizes that Heimdall is right - his power <i>has</i> been halved!&nbsp; He cries out to his father, but to no avail!\n\nLoki then realizes that this is his chance to strike, while Thor is at a disadvantage.&nbsp; But he knows it can't be a direct strike, or Odin will know.&nbsp; He must use another.&nbsp; He searches the centuries and finds Zarrko, the Tomorrow Man (<i>we saw him in [LINK:1962:JIM_86_thor0|JIM #86|2026-05-01], remember</i>?).&nbsp; Thor took away his memory of their encounter, but Loki restores it.\n\nZarrko is incensed that he suddenly finds himself working a menial job and rushes to return to the 20<sup>th</sup> century, this time taking a modified mining robot with him.&nbsp; When he arrives in the 20<sup>th</sup> century, the robot turns the police guns to dust!&nbsp; He raises a ruckus in town to attract Thor, and sure enough, Don Blake sees it on the news and hurries off to meet him as Thor, even knowing that his strength has been reduced by half.\n\nHe sees the robot tossing a building down on the road to block it, but when Thor crashes his hammer down to bash it into pieces, it doesn't break up!&nbsp; His power is definitely reduced!&nbsp; He tussles with the robot and holds his own for a good while before he finally is hurled backward.&nbsp; Zarrko offers to let him return to the 23<sup>rd</sup> century and serve him rather than let the robot go on a rampage, destroying the city!\n\nThor decides that if <i>he</i> can't defeat the robot with only half his strength, <i>nobody</i> can, and he agrees to Zarrko's terms.&nbsp; Zarrko is thrilled.&nbsp; He'll now have Thor helping him take over the 23<sup>rd</sup> century!&nbsp; They leave together.\n\nMeanwhile, back on Asgard, Odin and Loki watch, and not realizing <i>why</i> Thor has left the 20<sup>th</sup> century, Odin thinks that Thor has surrendered to a mortal!&nbsp; Loki is triumphant because if Thor helps Zarrko in the 23<sup>rd</sup> century, then Odin will never forgive him, never realizing that Loki was behind all of it!\n\n[CONTINUED]",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "An <i>excellent</i> issue, which came as a bit of a shock at first when I saw the cover and realized that the Tomorrow Man would be returning, since my recollection of him was that he was pretty lame.&nbsp; Except, <i>heh</i>, right off the bat, a laugh upon seeing the cover with the guy in the lower-left corner, showing an up-in-your-face horror look - <i>hahahaha</i>!&nbsp; Such <i>classic </i>Kirby!\n\nThor's mood at the beginning was a <i>bit</i> of a problem - he was miffed, I get it, but to be so single-minded as to allow himself to become a rampaging madman through the city - it's no <i>wonder</i> the Avengers felt the need to get out there to try and talk some sense into him.&nbsp; That was <i>good</i>, but I felt they gave up too soon.&nbsp; I get it - there's only so many pages you can use, but that seemed a bit much, and it prevented the book from grading higher.\n\nOdin, Loki, Heimdall were <i>all</i> completely in character, and it was great!&nbsp; Thor realizing that he is being punished was a bit heart-breaking even (with an absolutely <i>excellent</i> panel at the top-right of page 7), and seeing him struggle with the reduction in power was good.&nbsp; I think he gave up a bit too quickly at the end (probably for space) when he could have at least summoned his Avengers buddies for help, but that probably didn't seem god-like for him, particularly when he had already told them to get lost earlier.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nSure, the couple of little negatives ultimately prevented a full VF grade, but overall, this was a surprisingly solid issue, and I found myself turning the pages eagerly!&nbsp; A well done story!\n\nThe inking for this issue over Kirby's pencils by \"G. Bell\" is starkly different, with more emphasis on thin lines around the face.&nbsp; Still obviously Kirby, but it is interesting how different inking affects the overall look.",
  "side_note": [
    "The back-up story called \"The Enemies\" features a race called the \"Vulcans,\" a \"brutal race of ruthless warriors!\"&nbsp; What's ironic is that they face a race called the \"Idylicans,\" and it's <b><i>they</i></b> who have the pointed ears!&nbsp; <i>Heh</i>...",
    "<b>Tales of Asgard:&nbsp; </b>\"The Invasion of Asgard\"\n\nThis is more of the same as the last issue, as Loki tries to prevent the boyhood Thor from doing valiant deeds that allow him to slowly gain enough strength to finally lift the hammer of Odin.&nbsp; Loki is clearly more malevolent in this issue, but in the end, Thor is rescued by Odin's forces and is victorious, despite the undiscovered treachery of his brother."
  ],
